\section{Uncertainty Quantification Comparison}
\label{app:uncertainty}

\vspace{-16pt}
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

\looseness=-1
Across six METABENCH benchmarks, 17.9--31.3\% of items exhibit temporal DIF between 2023 and 2024 cohorts (${\approx}$27--29\% for MMLU after non-independence corrections; conservative bound: 13.5\%). Web-overlap labels are near-independent of DIF flags (Jaccard $= 0.206$); rankings are preserved ($\rho \approx 0.997$), motivating routine DIF audits.

\section*{Limitations}

\looseness=-1
Three primary limitations apply. (1)~The semi-synthetic framework uses uniform binary response flipping, a worst-case injection model; reported $\Delta\mathrm{TPR}$ values are upper-bound estimates, and detection power under realistic contamination patterns (partial memorization, format-specific advantages) is untested. More fundamentally, DIF detects \emph{that} items function differently but cannot determine \emph{why}. (2)~The MH $\chi^2$ test assumes independent examinees; LLM model families violate this assumption. Three convergent corrections (${\approx}$27--29\%) and family-level cluster bootstrap (95\% CI: $[26.7\%, 53.2\%]$) bound the impact, but the extreme relatedness of some families (e.g., hundreds of Llama-2 fine-tunes) exceeds the clustering levels studied in educational settings~\citep{french2010hierarchical}. (3)~Cross-subject matching assumes contamination affects a minority of domains; $\Delta\mathrm{FPR}$ rises to 0.069 when contamination spans ${\geq}20$ subjects. Temporal cohorts defined by calendar year provide coarse granularity, and generalizability to open-ended or partial-credit benchmarks is untested. Multidimensional DIF~\citep{ackerman1992didmf} may arise when MMLU's 57 subjects span distinct latent dimensions; cross-subject matching mitigates between-domain multidimensionality but not within-domain violations. IRT parameters are used only for auxiliary power analysis; all core DIF results are nonparametric.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\enlargethispage*{20\baselineskip}

\subsection{What Drives Temporal DIF?}
\label{sec:disc_temporal}

\looseness=-1
Temporal DIF detects \emph{that} items function differently across cohorts, not \emph{why}. Three non-exclusive mechanisms may drive the observed ${\approx}$27--29\% rate: (1)~differential contamination; (2)~capability evolution; (3)~training methodology shifts. The ETS baseline of 5--15\% C\%~\citep{clauser1998dif} is for concurrent demographic groups; the comparison to temporal LLM cohorts is illustrative, not normative.

\looseness=-1
Four exclusion arguments constrain this ambiguity: ability confounds explain at most 2.4~pp (matched C\% $= 28.9\%$; \Cref{sec:temporal}); domain-level improvement explains ${<}0.1\%$ of item-level DIF variance ($R^2 = 0.0007$); no enrichment among expert-annotated errors~\citep{gema2024mmlu} ($= 1.10$, $p = 0.23$); web-overlap labels~\citep{li2024contamination} are near-independent (Jaccard $= 0.206$; \Cref{sec:groundtruth}).

\looseness=-1
The aggregate difficulty--direction interaction ($\rho = -0.597$, robust at $[0.2, 0.8]$: $\rho = -0.447$, $p < 10^{-100}$; \Cref{sec:temporal}) is suggestive but not diagnostic: semi-synthetic analysis (\Cref{sec:semisynthetic}) shows uniform injection also produces $\rho \approx -0.52$ via ceiling effects, while difficulty-dependent injection yields $\rho \approx +0.77$ (zero overlap across seeds); the observed $\rho$ cannot alone distinguish contamination from capability evolution~\citep{suk2026rethinking}. The aggregate $\rho \approx 0.997$ masks subject-level instability (22/57 below 0.95; five below 0.90), motivating targeted replacement~\citep{zhao2025mmlucf,habba2026growingpains}.

\looseness=-1
Stratifying by model type reveals distinct mechanisms: base models ($N{=}1{,}409$) are 60.1\% reference-favoring with a strong difficulty gradient ($\rho = -0.570$), consistent with differential memorization decay, while instruct models ($N{=}478$) are 65.6\% focal-favoring with a near-zero gradient ($\rho = -0.079$; $\chi^2 = 562.7$, $p < 10^{-124}$), consistent with uniform alignment improvements. The aggregate gradient is driven by base models; the instruct flat gradient is compatible with either capability gains or difficulty-independent memorization. This decomposition provides positive evidence for mechanism separation, complementing the exclusion arguments above.

\subsection{Recommended DIF Audit Protocol}
\label{sec:disc_protocol}

\looseness=-1
Item-level properties do not predict DIF susceptibility (AUROC $= 0.60$; \Cref{app:predictive}), so monitoring cannot be replaced by pre-screening. We recommend MH-DIF audits whenever $\geq$500 models accumulate (ensuring $\geq$80 per stratum at $K{=}5$; \Cref{sec:validation}), with three action levels (calibrate via $\rho$-degradation; \Cref{app:fig_rho_degradation}): \emph{Monitor} (C\% $< 15\%$), \emph{Flag} ($15$--$25\%$: dual rankings), \emph{Act} ($\geq 25\%$: stable-item rankings). Difficulty--direction and model-type signals (\Cref{sec:disc_temporal}) inform triage; DIF removal is the least disruptive strategy (\Cref{app:fig_downstream}), motivating equating~\citep{kolen2014equating,habba2026growingpains}. Subject-level monitoring is essential (22/57 below $\rho = 0.95$).

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix~\citep{kipnis2024metabench} (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items~\citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}), with temporal cohorts defined by release date (2023 vs.\ 2024). Cross-benchmark validation across all six METABENCH benchmarks appears in \Cref{sec:gsm8k}.

\subsection{Validation: DIF Detection Power}
\label{sec:validation}

\looseness=-1
As approximate parametric bounds, a Monte Carlo power analysis (1,000 replications per condition) using empirical 2PL parameters from PSN-IRT~\citep{zhou2026psnirt} ($\bar{a} = 1.2$, $\bar{b} = -0.84$; 14,042 MMLU items) confirms that at $N \geq 80$ per cohort, ETS~C achieves TPR of 0.68--0.80, FPR of 0.036--0.042, and AUC of 0.93--0.94 for items with $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.4$ (details in \Cref{app:method_details}). Our temporal cohorts ($N_{\mathrm{ref}} = 1{,}080$, $N_{\mathrm{focal}} = 807$) far exceed this threshold. Power varies substantially with item discrimination: TPR ranges from 0.40 at $a = 0.5$ to 0.95 at $a = 2.0$; the gap between TPR at mean $a$ (0.87) and the aggregate TPR (0.68) quantifies the power cost of parameter heterogeneity, consistent with Jensen's inequality (\Cref{app:power_sensitivity}).

\subsection{Temporal Comparability: 31\% Measurement Instability}
\label{sec:temporal}

\looseness=-1
We compare 2023 ($N = 1{,}080$) against 2024 ($N = 807$) models; 31.3\% of items receive ETS~C classification ($|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$; 95\% bootstrap CI: [29.4\%, 38.1\%]; family-level cluster bootstrap CI $[26.7\%, 53.2\%]$). Two independent non-independence corrections converge on approximately 27--29\%: simple-mean DEFF correction (29.2\%) and family-level cluster bootstrap lower bound (26.7\%). The weighted-mean DEFF variant yields 13.5\%, but its effective cluster size ($\bar{m}{=}254$) implies each family member is functionally identical---unrealistic: within-family similarity is flat across family sizes ($\rho_s = -0.009$, $p = 0.96$; \Cref{app:icc_family}), so the weighted variant over-corrects. The $[13.5\%, 29.2\%]$ range reflects power uncertainty, not DIF magnitude disagreement ($|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$ unchanged). We adopt ${\approx}$27--29\% as our primary estimate, with 13.5\% as a lower bound under weighted-mean DEFF (\Cref{app:deff,app:uncertainty}); even this bound exceeds the random-split baseline by $75\times$. Leave-one-family-out analysis confirms no single family drives the signal ($|\Delta\mathrm{C\%}| \leq 2.93$\,pp). Graduated de-duplication independently confirms C\% scales with power, not family redundancy: at each fixed $K$, within-$K$ SD ${<}1.5$\,pp across random draws (\Cref{app:dedup_k}). C\% is stable across $K \geq 5$ (31.3\%--35.9\%; $K{=}5$ most conservative); the non-monotonic $K{=}10$ peak reflects ability--DIF interactions in finer strata, consistent with residual non-uniform DIF that MH does not capture, while $K{=}3$ inflates to 39.4\% from under-stratification.

\looseness=-1
Three controls confirm genuine temporal change (\Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}): random splits yield C\% of 0.18\% (2023) and 1.3\% (2024), both ${\geq}23\times$ below temporal C\%; ability-quintile comparison yields 2.9\%; and 1:1 nearest-neighbor matching on total score ($N{=}638$/group, Cohen's $d = -0.17$) yields C\% $= 28.9\% \pm 0.1\%$, confirming at most ${\sim}2.4$~pp is attributable to ability mismatch ($p < 0.01$; \Cref{app:method_details}).

\looseness=-1
Across all 57 subjects, C\% ranges from 20.7\% to 48.4\% (mean $\approx 30\%$; \Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}). DIF rates are domain-invariant (Kruskal-Wallis $p = 0.78$), but DIF \emph{direction} exhibits domain dependence: STEM items favor 2024 models (55.4\%), Humanities favor 2023 (64.4\%). Domain-level accuracy gains explain ${<}0.1\%$ of item-level DIF variance (pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$); a Simpson's paradox confirms DIF is associated with item properties rather than aggregate gains (OR reverses from 1.55 to 0.73 after controlling for difficulty; \Cref{app:structural}). A difficulty--direction interaction emerges ($\rho = -0.597$): 2024 models gain on hard items while eroding on easy ones. The conclusion is robust across thresholds ($18.3\%$ at $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 2.0$, $10.2\%$ at $\geq 2.5$).

